{0 Stage 1 Rocq/OCaml Arbitration}

This page records the case-by-case arbitration for Stage 1 adequacy between
the existing Rocq formalization and the current OCaml implementation.

The goal is not to make OCaml mimic Rocq mechanically. The OCaml
implementation is the tested implementation of Kairos and may reveal that the
Rocq model should be refined. Conversely, when OCaml only hides a
proof-relevant object behind a backend view, OCaml should expose it more
clearly.

{1 Decision Rule}

For each mismatch, decide one of:

- {b Rocq -> OCaml}: OCaml is missing a proof-relevant principle that Rocq
  models correctly;
- {b OCaml -> Rocq}: the tested implementation expresses the intended Kairos
  principle and Rocq should be refined to match it;
- {b expose}: OCaml already has the principle but should expose it at the
  canonical boundary;
- {b backend}: the mismatch is an optimization or representation choice after
  canonical obligations;
- {b presentation}: the mismatch is mostly naming, comments, or paper
  exposition.

No code generation rule should be changed until the corresponding row has a
clear direction and a regression plan.

{1 Current Stage 1 Objects}

Rocq Stage 1 is centered on:

- [kairosSummary] and [kairosSummariesFromSourceAndContracts] in
  [theories/Compilation/Stage1/Construction/SummaryGeneration.v];
- [ProofStepSummary], [KernelClause], and [SummaryClauseFamilies] in
  [theories/Obligations/Semantics/StepContracts.v].

OCaml Stage 1 is currently centered on:

- [From_model.build_minimal_summaries], which builds grouped
  [Ir.product_step_summary] values from explored product steps;
- [Pre.run_node] and [Post.run_node], which add canonical requirements and
  postconditions to summaries;
- [Canonical_obligations.build_stage1], which exposes the canonical Stage 1
  boundary;
- [Kernel_clause_projection.build], which exposes classified kernel clauses
  before proof export and Why3 lowering.

{1 Arbitration}

{2 A1. Candidate Set}

Rocq:

- enumerates all indexed program transitions, including implicit defaults;
- crosses them with all assumption and guarantee automaton transitions;
- filters by class and summary relevance.

OCaml:

- explores the product from the initial product state;
- groups only product steps discovered by that exploration;
- keeps relevant safe and bad-guarantee cases.

Assessment:

The OCaml choice is not a backend optimization. It is the operationally useful
proof principle: obligations are needed for product states reachable from the
initial state under the product construction. Generating obligations for live
but structurally unreachable product states is not necessary for run
soundness.

Direction:

{b OCaml -> Rocq}, unless a later proof shows that Rocq's global enumeration
is merely a harmless superset used for proof convenience. The paper should
present reachable product construction, or explicitly state the equivalence
between the global enumeration and the reachable subset used by Kairos.

Risk:

Changing OCaml toward global enumeration would likely increase obligations and
runtime without improving the intended theorem.

{2 A2. Summary Grouping}

Rocq:

- builds singleton summaries;
- groups summaries by summary identity;
- despite names such as [kairosGroupSafeSingletons], the current construction
  effectively groups relevant summaries by identity and carries both
  [pssSafeCases] and [pssUnsafeCases].

OCaml:

- groups product steps by program transition, product source, and assumption
  edge;
- stores all safe cases and bad-guarantee cases in the grouped summary.

Assessment:

The mathematical shape is close. The main problem is Rocq naming and comments:
the construction is not purely "safe grouping" if unsafe cases are carried in
the same summary shape.

Direction:

{b presentation} first, possibly {b OCaml -> Rocq} for names/comments. Do not
change OCaml grouping.

Risk:

Splitting OCaml summaries just to match misleading Rocq names would disturb a
tested and useful grouping invariant.

{2 A3. Requirements And Entry Clauses}

Rocq:

- [pssRequires] contains source invariant, propagation requirements, assumption
  guard, program guard, and stability facts;
- [pssEntryClauses] contains one [kairosEntryRequiresClause] per requirement;
- [isPhasePreClause] classifies exactly those entry clauses.

OCaml:

- [Pre.run_node] constructs the same categories in [summary.requires];
- Stage 2 and Why3 use those requirements directly as contract preconditions;
- [Canonical_obligations.build_stage1] does not currently expose a separate
  entry-clause family.

Assessment:

Entry clauses are not external VCs in the OCaml pipeline. They are a Rocq
classification device around facts that become preconditions. The important
semantic object is [requires], not an extra obligation with empty hypotheses.

Direction:

{b OCaml -> Rocq} for the principle: requirements should be presented as
contract preconditions. If Rocq needs [pssEntryClauses] internally, keep them
as a proof device, but do not force OCaml to generate new obligations. OCaml
may add a derived entry-clause view only if it helps the adequacy audit.

Risk:

Adding unconditional entry VCs to OCaml would be wrong: requirements are
assumptions of local step obligations, not facts to prove globally in
isolation.

{2 A4. Source Clauses And Phase Facts}

Rocq:

- [kairosSourceSummaryClauses] publishes an exclusive source/phase formula for
  a product state;
- it filters formulas that mention current inputs.

OCaml:

- [Kernel_clause_projection.build_source_summary_clauses] computes source
  phase facts from incoming safe summaries;
- current-input mentions are rejected through [Fo_current_input] as a static
  invariant, not silently filtered.

Assessment:

The source-clause shape is close. The current-input discipline should be a
well-formedness/static side condition, not a semantic deletion of clauses.
That matches the earlier correction: invariants and propagated facts must not
silently drop formulas because they mention current inputs.

Direction:

{b OCaml -> Rocq} for the current-input discipline. Rocq should model the
well-formedness condition, or state the side condition explicitly, rather than
filter source clauses semantically.

Risk:

Keeping semantic filtering in Rocq would hide bad formulas instead of rejecting
them.

{2 A5. Phase Clauses}

Rocq:

- emits [kairosPhaseClause] for every formula in [pssEnsures].

OCaml:

- emits [PhaseStepSummary] for the guarantee automaton guard of the product
  step;
- emits [PhaseStepPreSummary] from a compatibility/source-phase formula;
- does not treat every postcondition in [summary.ensures] as a phase fact.

Assessment:

This is a real conceptual mismatch. In OCaml, "ensures" are local
postconditions of the step contract, while phase facts are a smaller temporal
classification used for product/automaton propagation. Rocq currently appears
to mix these roles by using every [pssEnsures] formula as a phase clause.

Direction:

Likely {b OCaml -> Rocq}. The paper calculus should distinguish:

- postconditions used by step contracts;
- phase/source facts used to propagate product-state knowledge.

Before modifying Rocq, audit which lemmas rely on [kairosPhaseClause] over all
[pssEnsures]. If those lemmas only need a smaller phase-fact subset, Rocq
should be refined. If they really need all ensures, we must identify the OCaml
counterpart instead of adding backend-specific clauses blindly.

This audit is now recorded in {!page-stage1_phase_facts_audit}. Its conclusion
is that run-level soundness uses selected [pssEnsures] subfamilies as
postconditions transported across ticks, not [isPhaseClause] itself. Rocq
should therefore split postconditions from phase/progression facts instead of
making every ensure a phase clause.

Risk:

Changing OCaml to classify every ensure as a phase fact would conflate local
postconditions with temporal product facts and may reintroduce invalid shifted
facts.

{2 A6. Propagation Clauses}

Rocq:

- emits one node-propagation clause and one automaton-propagation clause from
  base hypotheses;
- carries destination program state, destination state invariants, and
  destination guarantee automaton state.

OCaml:

- emits [PropagationNodeInvariant] and [PropagationAutomatonCoherence] with
  the same broad shape;
- emits them only for live source states according to the product state
  predicate passed to [Kernel_clause_projection.build].

Assessment:

The clause shape is close. The remaining issue is not the propagation clause
itself; it is the reachable/live product domain over which clauses are
generated.

Direction:

{b expose} plus {b OCaml -> Rocq} for the reachable-domain story. Keep OCaml
generation as is; make the domain side condition explicit in the paper/Rocq
model.

Risk:

None if treated as a domain restriction. High if treated as arbitrary pruning.

{2 A7. Safety Clauses}

Rocq:

- emits one safety clause per top-level disjunct of each bad-guarantee guard.

OCaml:

- [Kernel_clause_projection] also splits top-level disjunctions in bad
  guarantee guards;
- [Canonical_obligations.build_stage2] keeps one Stage 2 unsafe contract per
  unsafe case, while backend views may split/group later.

Assessment:

Stage 1 safety clauses are close. Stage 2 is now correctly separated: the
canonical contract granularity is one unsafe case; splitting and grouping are
backend-preservation issues.

Direction:

{b match} for Stage 1 safety clauses modulo representation. {b backend} for
later splitting/grouping.

Risk:

Do not let Stage 1 splitting be mistaken for Stage 2 contract granularity.

{2 A8. Initialization}

Rocq:

- creates a global initialization summary containing init node and automaton
  clauses;
- [kairosInitInvariantGoals] is currently empty.

OCaml:

- [Kernel_clause_projection.build] emits initialization clauses directly;
- [Pre.run_node] leaves initial invariant goals empty;
- frontend/model validation rejects ordinary invariants on the initial state.

Assessment:

The mathematical rule is aligned: no ordinary state invariant has to be
re-established at the initial state. The representation differs: Rocq wraps
init clauses in a synthetic summary, OCaml treats them as initialization
clauses.

Direction:

{b OCaml -> Rocq} for presentation: init obligations should be a separate
family, not necessarily a fake product-step summary. Rocq may keep the global
summary internally if convenient, but the paper should not present it as an
ordinary step summary.

Risk:

Low. This is mostly representation.

{2 A9. Product Characteristics / Propagation Requires}

Rocq:

- [kairosPropagationRequiresForSrc] is built from incoming safe guards, with
  the initial product state adding [true].

OCaml:

- [Product_characteristics] computes entry and post facts from incoming safe
  cases;
- for sources involved in bad-guarantee reasoning, it may use stronger
  program-characteristic facts that include program guards and shifted input
  information.

Assessment:

This is not a backend optimization. It is part of making local deductive
obligations strong enough while preserving correctness. The fact that the
medical example needs historical/product facts is evidence that the
mathematical model should include the general product-characteristic
principle, not only raw incoming guarantee guards.

Direction:

{b OCaml -> Rocq}, after extracting the general principle from
[Product_characteristics] instead of formalizing medical-specific facts.

Risk:

If Rocq keeps the weaker incoming-guard-only model, it may validate a proof
principle that is too weak to explain the implementation.

{1 Immediate Conclusions}

1. Do not change OCaml Stage 1 generation just to match Rocq names.
2. Treat reachable product construction as the intended implementation
   principle unless proven otherwise.
3. Treat entry clauses as Rocq proof/classification structure around
   requirements, not as new OCaml VCs.
4. Separate phase facts from step-contract postconditions. This is the most
   important conceptual Stage 1 mismatch.
5. Move the current-input restriction in Rocq from semantic filtering to a
   well-formedness/static side condition.
6. Model product characteristics as a general principle, not as an
   optimization and not as a medical-specific trick.

{1 Proposed Next Work}

The phase-fact audit is recorded in {!page-stage1_phase_facts_audit}. The next
work item is not an OCaml refactor. It is Rocq-side design:

- decide whether Rocq should split [pssEnsures] into postconditions and phase
  facts, or add a separate phase-fact projection;
- only after that, decide whether [Canonical_obligations.stage1] needs a
  richer field to expose phase facts explicitly.
